# Deployment configuration and secrets for the Raspberry Pi stack.
#
# Copy this to /opt/gleanvolt/.env ON THE PI and fill it in -- it never leaves that machine and is not
# committed (the repo's .gitignore covers "*.env"). deploy.sh does not copy it.
#
#   scp deploy/.env.example martin@192.168.2.7:/opt/gleanvolt/.env
#   ssh martin@192.168.2.7 'chmod 600 /opt/gleanvolt/.env && nano /opt/gleanvolt/.env'
#
# docker compose reads this file for the ${...} substitutions in docker-compose.yml. Which of Home
# Assistant and the broker actually start is decided by which deploy script you run, not by anything
# in here -- see deploy/README.md's "Running without Home Assistant" for the combinations this makes
# possible and their memory budgets.
#
# Only the "Site" block below has to be right for your installation. Everything else has a working
# default, including the web UI, which is on whether or not this file mentions it.

# --- Image -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One package covers every platform (arm64 here, amd64 and Windows elsewhere) as a multi-platform
# manifest list, so this tag never needs an architecture in it -- docker picks the right image.
#
#   latest        newest build of main
#   1.0.0         a released version. NOTE: no "v", though the git tag is v1.0.0 -- the publish
#                 workflow strips the prefix. IMAGE_TAG=v1.0.0 fails with "manifest unknown".
#   sha-abc1234   one specific build, immutable -- what to pin to when rolling back
#
#   IMAGE_TAG=sha-abc1234 ./deploy/deploy.sh
#
# Only if a release is held up by one platform failing to build: the single-platform tags are
# published regardless, so IMAGE_TAG=sha-abc1234-linux-arm64 still gets this Pi running.
IMAGE_TAG=latest

# --- Site --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both devices speak Modbus TCP on port 502, so a reachability check passes even when these two are
# the wrong way round -- and the symptoms of that are strange rather than obvious: SOC frozen at some
# constant, solar and grid stuck at 0, the charger permanently "Unknown". Confirm which is which
# before trusting either line. The charger serves its own web UI whose page identifies it:
#
#   curl -s http://<address>/ | head -20      # the EV charger's page mentions "chargeweb"
#
# Or read one register from each: the inverter reports a believable battery SOC at 0x1C, and it
# should match the figure on the inverter's own display.
TZ=Europe/Prague
INVERTER_HOST=192.168.2.10
EV_CHARGER_HOST=192.168.2.6

# --- Your PV system ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this installation is, in one place (issue #111): what to call it, where it is, and what the
# array does. None of it is required -- the controller runs on the two addresses above alone -- but
# each value buys something specific, noted beside it.
#
# Everything here is optional and unset means unset, never zero: 0,0 is a real place in the Atlantic
# and a zero-capacity array is not a defaulted one.
#
#PV_ID=home-roof                      # a slug; becomes this system's name on the broker (a later phase)
#PV_NAME=Home Roof                    # what a human sees, in the web UI and Home Assistant
#PV_ADDRESS=Street 1, Town, Country   # display only, never parsed
#PV_LATITUDE=49.267803                # with PV_LONGITUDE: the site the weather below is fetched for
#PV_LONGITUDE=16.529486
#PV_AZIMUTH=180                       # compass bearing: 0 north, 90 east, 180 south. -180 is accepted
#PV_TILT=15                           # degrees from horizontal; 0 flat, 90 vertical
#PV_CAPACITY_KWP=8.5                  # peak DC capacity of the array
#PV_INVERTER_CAPACITY_KW=8            # AC side, where it is smaller than the array
#PV_LOSS_FACTOR=0.9                   # fraction of DC yield that reaches the meter
#PV_INSTALL_DATE=2026-04-01           # yyyy-MM-dd
#
# What the boxes are. Documentation, not a selector: the register maps are compiled for these two
# devices, so naming another model here mislabels the one the controller is talking to rather than
# changing how it talks to it.
#PV_INVERTER_MODEL=SolaX X3-HYB-G4 PRO
#PV_CHARGER_MODEL=SolaX X1/X3-HAC
#PV_CHARGER_ID=charger
#
# PV_ID is what every MQTT topic is namespaced by, so two installations can share one broker. It is
# **required** once HOMEASSISTANT_ENABLED=true below; a controller-only deployment publishes nothing
# and needs none.

# --- Home Assistant + MQTT broker (optional) ----------------------------------------------------
# Both are opt-in: the controller runs, and can be fully controlled from its own web UI below, with
# neither. Which one you get is a choice of *script*, not a setting here:
#
#   ./deploy/deploy.sh                    -- full stack: controller, mosquitto, Home Assistant
#   ./deploy/deploy-controller-only.sh    -- controller alone (+ web UI, if enabled below)
#
# deploy.sh (full stack) still needs two settings to agree with it:
#
#   1. HOMEASSISTANT_ENABLED=true          -- the controller's own switch to publish MQTT
#   2. MQTT_USERNAME / MQTT_PASSWORD below -- must match the broker's password file (README step 7)
#
# **Upgrading from a deployment that predates deploy-controller-only.sh?** Nothing changes -- keep
# running deploy.sh and set HOMEASSISTANT_ENABLED=true below, exactly as before. (If you manage
# `docker compose` directly on the Pi rather than through either script, COMPOSE_PROFILES=
# mosquitto,homeassistant is what decides it there instead.)
#HOMEASSISTANT_ENABLED=true

# Must match the credentials in the broker's password file. Create that file with:
#   docker run --rm -v /opt/gleanvolt/mosquitto/config:/mosquitto/config eclipse-mosquitto:2 \
#     mosquitto_passwd -c -b /mosquitto/config/passwd solax '<password>'
MQTT_USERNAME=solax
MQTT_PASSWORD=

# What Home Assistant keys every entity by -- and therefore what its history hangs off. Left alone,
# docker-compose.yml pins `solax_controller`, which is what every deployment that already exists was
# created with; a new one may set it to the PV id instead, or to empty, which means the same thing.
#
# CHANGING IT DISCARDS THE HISTORY: Home Assistant sees new unique_ids, creates sensor.*_2 alongside
# the old entities and starts every graph again. Topics, the device name and the device page all
# follow PV_ID freely and cost nothing -- this one does not. If you do change it, set
# HA_RETIRE_DEVICE_ID to the old value in the same deploy so the retained configs left on the broker
# are cleared instead of resurrecting yesterday's device. See the README's "Renaming what Home
# Assistant sees" for the procedure that keeps the history.
#HA_DEVICE_ID=solax_controller
#HA_RETIRE_DEVICE_ID=

# Retained state left under a prefix this system no longer publishes on. Upgrading from a release that
# predates PV_ID? Set this to "solax/solax_controller" for one deploy, then remove it again. It
# changes nothing functional -- it is the difference between a broker that shows one of everything and
# one that shows two.
#HA_RETIRE_TOPIC_PREFIX=

# --- The car (optional) --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is on the other end of the cable. Separate from "Vehicle telemetry" below, which describes the
# *feed* that reports on it -- the broker, the credentials, how stale a reading may be. These describe
# the car itself, and nothing here is required: every figure falls back to the installation's, which
# is exactly how the controller behaved before this section existed.
#
# Identity. EV_ID is a slug (lower-case letters, digits, '-' and '_'); the rest are reported and never
# acted on -- the day a model string selects behaviour is the day a typo changes how a car is charged.
#EV_ID=id4
#EV_NAME=The ID.4
#EV_MAKE=Volkswagen
#EV_MODEL=ID.4 Pro

# The car's *usable* battery, in kWh -- the figure its own SOC is a percentage of, not the gross pack
# on the brochure (an ID.4 Pro is 77 usable of 82 gross). Set it to ask the targeted plan for a state
# of charge -- "80% by seven" -- instead of an amount of energy; leave it unset and that basis is
# simply not offered, which is the only thing it affects.
#VEHICLE_BATTERY_CAPACITY_KWH=77
# How much of what the charger meters reaches the cells. Applied to a battery-target request, since
# the promise is metered at the charger. Nothing to do with the home battery's charge efficiency.
#VEHICLE_CHARGE_EFFICIENCY=0.9

# What the CAR can take -- not what the wallbox offers. The controller works to the narrower of the
# two, always, and a car can only ever lower a limit.
#
# > EV_PHASES is the one that goes wrong quietly. Every watts<->amps conversion runs through a phase
# > count. A single-phase car behind a three-phase wallbox that does not say so has every power figure
# > the controller reasons with overstated threefold: a deferred fast charge starts hours late and the
# > day plan budgets energy the car can never take. Nothing looks broken; the numbers are simply wrong.
#
# EV_MIN_CHARGING_CURRENT_AMPS matters for a car that refuses low currents: commanded 6 A when it needs
# 8 it draws nothing at all, and a connected car taking no power is what the fast mode reads as
# *finished*. A car whose minimum is above the installation's maximum is refused at startup, naming
# both keys, rather than discovered one evening as "I pressed the button and nothing happened".
#
# The three-phase VW ID.4 values, which are the defaults these examples assume:
#EV_PHASES=3
#EV_MIN_CHARGING_CURRENT_AMPS=6
#EV_MAX_CHARGING_CURRENT_AMPS=16

# Note the two names above that still read VEHICLE_* rather than EV_*: they are unchanged from when
# these settings lived under the Vehicle section, so an existing .env keeps working untouched. Only
# the configuration key they feed has moved.

# --- Vehicle telemetry (optional) ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Reads the EV's *own* battery SOC -- as distinct from the home battery, and from the charger's view
# of what is plugged into it. Read-only: no charge decision depends on it, and it is not republished
# to Home Assistant, which is where it comes from.
#
# The controller subscribes to one topic with one JSON schema; a template in Home Assistant adapts
# your car onto it, which is why a second vehicle costs no code. See the README's "Vehicle telemetry"
# section for the automation to paste into Home Assistant.
#
# Three things have to agree:
#
#   1. VEHICLE_ENABLED=true                -- the controller's own switch
#   2. VEHICLE_TOPIC                       -- must match what your HA automation publishes to
#   3. MQTT_USERNAME / MQTT_PASSWORD above -- the same broker credentials as Home Assistant uses
#
#VEHICLE_ENABLED=true
#VEHICLE_TOPIC=gleanvolt/vehicle/id4/state

# Only if the broker is not the `mosquitto` service on this compose network -- e.g. under
# deploy-controller-only.sh, where Home Assistant and its broker live on another machine.
#VEHICLE_BROKER_HOST=192.168.2.7
#VEHICLE_BROKER_PORT=1883

# How old a reading may be before it is shown as stale. Twelve hours sounds enormous and is not: a
# parked car's SOC does not drift, so an overnight reading is exactly correct. This is sized to notice
# a *dead feed* -- an expired cloud session, a broken automation -- rather than to reject old numbers.
#VEHICLE_MAX_AGE=12:00:00

# Ask the car only when somebody wants to know -- a person pressing "Ask the car" on the dashboard, or
# a plan being prepared -- rather than on a clock. Off by default; on, nothing polls.
#
# Why it is worth having: on the EU Data Act portal a reading is 2-4 hours behind, because VW's
# pipeline sits on each report the car makes (measured across a 7h43m window: best 1h48m, worst 7h16m).
# A card showing that number continuously is confident and wrong, and the eye reads the figure rather
# than the age beside it.
#
# The cost: nothing polls, so no state of charge is recorded against a charging session. On a feed
# hours behind, those samples were the same pre-session number repeated rather than a charge curve --
# but turn this off if you want them back.
#VEHICLE_ON_DEMAND_ONLY=true

# --- volkswagen.de, the live source (optional) -----------------------------------------------------
# Polled ONLY while a charging session is open, so the car's state of charge is recorded as it moves.
# Nothing is fetched while idle.
#
# Why it exists beside the EU Data Act portal above: measured on 2026-09-05, this source reported a
# reading the car captured at 08:39 while the portal was still serving one from 21:16 the evening
# before. The portal publishes 1h48m to 7h16m behind the car, so a charge ends before its first
# in-charge reading appears there.
#
# A cold login ALWAYS wants a one-time code emailed to the account owner, so signing in is done from
# the web UI's Vehicle portal page while you are there to read it -- not from here, and never
# automatically. These settings only say which account and which car.
#
# The signed-in session is kept in data/vw-website-session.json (a bind mount, so it survives a
# restart) and carries the "remember this browser" grant. Treat that file as a password: whoever holds
# it is signed in as you.
#VW_WEBSITE_ENABLED=true
#VW_WEBSITE_USERNAME=you@example.com
#VW_WEBSITE_PASSWORD=
#VW_WEBSITE_VIN=WVGZZZ...
#VW_WEBSITE_POLL_INTERVAL=00:05:00

# --- The car from VW's own portal (optional, and beside or instead of the MQTT feed above) ----------
# For a VW Group car -- VW, Audi, Skoda, SEAT, Cupra, Bentley -- the controller can read the EU Data
# Act portal itself, with no Home Assistant in the middle. Free: the Data Act gives the *owner* their
# car's data, which is exactly why the owner's own login ends up being the credential.
#
# Two browser steps no setting replaces: accept the consent screen, and enable a continuous data
# request (Data clusters -> Vehicle overview -> Get customised data, "All data", 15 minutes). Without
# the second there is nothing to download however correct these are. Walkthrough:
# ../docs/VW_PORTAL_SETUP.md
#
# VW_PASSWORD IS NOT A READ-ONLY CREDENTIAL. The same account unlocks and locates the car, and there
# is no scoped token to use instead. What protects it is that this file is gitignored and the client
# never logs it, renders it, or writes it anywhere.
#VW_BRAND=vw
#VW_USERNAME=you@example.com
#VW_PASSWORD=
#VW_VIN=                      # only when the account can see more than one car

# The credentials above enable the web UI's "Vehicle portal" button. THIS switches on the feed: the
# same portal read every fifteen minutes -- the portal's own delivery frequency -- and written to the
# dashboard's vehicle card, plus a "Car feed" entity in Home Assistant that says Ok, Degraded or
# NeedsOwner.
#
# Press the button first. It is what proves the credentials and that this car's fields are understood,
# and it costs one sign-in rather than one every quarter of an hour.
#
# WITH THIS ON, BOTH FEEDS RUN and the freshest reading wins. This used to leave VEHICLE_ENABLED's
# MQTT feed unsubscribed -- one reading, one writer, no race -- until the reference car showed the
# portal's SOC to be coarser and later than the same manufacturer's app API arriving through Home
# Assistant. Which source is better is a fact about a car and a moment, not something this file can
# settle once. So both write to one reading, whichever saw the car most recently is kept, and the
# dashboard names which feed the reading on screen came from.
#
# RUN BOTH FOR A WEEK BEFORE CHOOSING. The web UI's /vehicle-feeds page counts what each one actually
# delivered -- cadence, agreement, coverage, and how many times the portal session had to be
# re-established. Then the handover is two settings and a stopped automation:
#
#   VW_ENABLED=true         # keep the portal feed
#   VEHICLE_ENABLED=false   # stop subscribing to the MQTT topic
#
# ...and stop the Home Assistant automation publishing to VEHICLE_TOPIC. Nothing is deleted: the MQTT
# feed stays in the build, so if the portal disappoints next month it is one line away -- and it is
# still the answer for any car nobody has written a service for.
#VW_ENABLED=true

# How many of the portal's deliveries one read may merge (default 4). The portal splits a car across
# report types, so a read merges older deliveries while the reading is still short of something.
# Raise it to reach further back for a field that has not appeared -- each delivery is one ZIP -- and
# put it back once you know whether that field is ever coming.
#VW_MAX_DATASETS=12

# --- Self-hosted web UI ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The controller's own dashboard -- see the root README's "Self-hosted web UI" section for what it
# shows and controls. **Nothing here needs setting.** It is on by default, on port 8090, with the
# port published by docker-compose.yml, so a deploy that touches none of these lines still ends at a
# working http://<pi>:8090. These exist to change that, not to arrange it.
#WEB_ENABLED=false     # no listening socket at all -- Home Assistant or nothing
#WEB_PORT=8090         # 8090 is both the host and the container port; changing this moves both

# --- Web UI password (advanced) -------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no login by default: the UI is open to anyone who can reach the port, which is the right
# default for a LAN appliance and the wrong one if that LAN has guests on it, or if the port is
# reachable from anywhere else. Setting a hash below is all it takes to require a sign-in -- there is
# no second switch to remember, and a password can never be set and then quietly not enforced.
#
# The value is the *hash*, never the password. Generate one with the image itself (no configuration
# and no listening socket involved; it prints the hash and exits):
#
#   docker run --rm ghcr.io/mpospisil/gleanvolt:latest hash-password '<your password>'
#
#WEB_PASSWORD_HASH=
#
# WEB_REQUIRE_AUTHENTICATION overrides that inference and is almost never needed. =true with no hash
# is refused at startup (nobody could sign in); =false serves the UI openly even though a hash is
# configured. Leave it unset unless you specifically want one of those.
#WEB_REQUIRE_AUTHENTICATION=true

# --- Solcast (optional) ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without these the worker logs a warning and skips forecast refreshes; everything else still runs.
SOLCAST_API_KEY=
SOLCAST_RESOURCE_ID=

# --- Weather (optional) ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Records the sky each charging session ran in -- once when it opens, once when it closes -- so a
# session can be read against the day it happened on rather than only against the forecast. Needs an
# OpenWeatherMap key (the free plan is enough: this is two calls per session) and PV_LATITUDE /
# PV_LONGITUDE above -- which site the sky is over is a fact about the site, not about the provider.
# Leave the key empty, or the coordinates unset, and no weather call is ever made.
WEATHER_API_KEY=

# --- Hardware-writing features -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Left at the shipped defaults deliberately: deploying must not start writing to your inverter or
# charger. The service always boots with charge control in mode Off and takes control only when you
# select a mode (Home Assistant or the web UI), so there is no "enable" flag for it -- only a dry
# run. Change these only after verifying the register addresses against your own device (see README).
#CHARGE_CONTROL_DRY_RUN=false
#BATTERY_HOLD_ENABLED=false
#BATTERY_HOLD_DRY_RUN=true

# --- Memory limits -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hard caps, not reservations: a container uses what it uses, and the cap only decides when the
# kernel kills it. Measured steady state on the reference 4 GB Pi is HA ~500 MB, controller ~75 MB,
# broker ~15 MB, so the defaults below sit well clear rather than just above -- headroom for HA's
# startup, updates and recorder purges. Only relevant to the services actually started (see
# deploy.sh vs. deploy-controller-only.sh above); a controller-only deployment needs none of it.
# Lower them on a board that cannot spare the ceiling. Requires cgroup memory accounting enabled in
# /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt -- see README.
#HA_MEM_LIMIT=1536m
#CONTROLLER_MEM_LIMIT=512m
#MOSQUITTO_MEM_LIMIT=48m
